Yep—if you want `/health` to stay plain HTTP (no redirect, no TLS), your updated Nginx config is basically right. Two tweaks I’d make:

1) have `/health` proxy to the app’s real `/health` (so it reflects app status, not just “nginx is up”)  
2) add the same proxy headers in both places

Here’s the version I’d use.

## `nginx/nginx.conf` (HTTP health, everything else redirects to HTTPS)
```nginx
events {}

http {
  upstream dashboard_app {
    server app:8000;
  }

  server {
    listen 80;
    server_name localhost;

    # HTTP-only health endpoint (no redirect)
    location = /health {
      proxy_pass http://dashboard_app/health;
      proxy_set_header Host $host;
      proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
      proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    }

    # Everything else -> HTTPS
    location / {
      return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
    }
  }

  server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name localhost;

    ssl_certificate     /etc/nginx/certs/dashboard.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/certs/dashboard.key;

    location / {
      proxy_pass http://dashboard_app;
      proxy_set_header Host $host;
      proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
      proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    }
  }
}
```

### Behavior
- `http://localhost/health` → hits FastAPI `/health` directly, stays HTTP, no redirect
- `http://localhost/anything-else` → 301 to HTTPS
- `https://localhost/` and `https://localhost/api/*` → proxied to FastAPI and still protected by Basic Auth

If you also want `/health` to be reachable via HTTPS *without* auth, we can add an explicit `location = /health` block in the 443 server and skip the auth at the app layer for that route (currently you’re leaving `/health` unprotected anyway, so it would work).

