Subdomain is the easiest, so let’s assume **`release-notes-api.company.com`** and you’re proxying to the container on the same host. Here’s a minimal NGINX config that plays nicely with Docker and (optionally) Basic Auth.

## NGINX config (subdomain → docker container)

If your compose maps `3000:3000` like you have, NGINX can proxy to `127.0.0.1:3000`:

```nginx
# /etc/nginx/sites-available/release-notes-api.conf

server {
  listen 80;
  server_name release-notes-api.company.com;

  # If you're using certbot, you can let certbot manage redirects instead.
  return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
}

server {
  listen 443 ssl http2;
  server_name release-notes-api.company.com;

  ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/release-notes-api.company.com/fullchain.pem;
  ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/release-notes-api.company.com/privkey.pem;

  # Good defaults if you have them
  include /etc/letsencrypt/options-ssl-nginx.conf;

  # Optional basic auth (remove if you have SSO upstream)
  # auth_basic "Release Notes API";
  # auth_basic_user_file /etc/nginx/.htpasswd;

  location / {
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000;

    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;

    # Helpful for keepalive + streaming
    proxy_http_version 1.1;
    proxy_set_header Connection "";

    # Optional timeouts
    proxy_connect_timeout 5s;
    proxy_read_timeout 30s;
    proxy_send_timeout 30s;
  }
}
```

Enable it:

```bash
sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/release-notes-api.conf /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/
sudo nginx -t
sudo systemctl reload nginx
```

## If you’d rather proxy to the container by name (cleaner)
Instead of publishing `3000:3000`, put NGINX in the same compose network and proxy to `http://release-notes-api:3000`. That avoids exposing port 3000 on the host at all. If you want, paste your current compose and I’ll show the exact tweak.

## Basic Auth file (optional)
```bash
sudo apt-get install apache2-utils
sudo htpasswd -c /etc/nginx/.htpasswd youruser
sudo nginx -t && sudo systemctl reload nginx
```

One thing I need from you to avoid a “works locally but 404s in prod” situation: is NGINX running **on the same machine** as the Docker container, or is it a separate VM/load balancer?

