Your current “detect `www.` links” logic won’t really work as written, because `a.href` is **always normalized to an absolute URL** by the browser (so it’ll typically start with `http(s)://` even if the author wrote something odd), and `markdown-it` with `linkify: true` may already turn `www.example.com` into a proper link depending on its linkify rules. The most reliable “nudge” is to scan the **raw markdown** you got from the API.

### Minimal approach: scan `note.markdown` and show a sidebar warning

1) **Add this to `index.html`** under the existing status line:

```html
<div id="warning" class="warning"></div>
```

2) **Add CSS**:

```css
.warning {
  color: #b45309;
  font-size: 12px;
  margin-top: 6px;
}
```

3) **Update `renderNote()`** (this version includes: render + sanitize + external links `_blank` + markdown scan warning):

```js
function renderNote(note) {
  $("title").textContent = note.title;
  $("meta").textContent = `${note.date} • ${note.slug}`;

  const unsafeHtml = md.render(note.markdown || "");
  const safeHtml = DOMPurify.sanitize(unsafeHtml);

  const container = $("content");
  container.innerHTML = safeHtml;

  // Only open absolute HTTP(S) links in a new tab
  container
    .querySelectorAll("a[href^='http://'], a[href^='https://']")
    .forEach(a => {
      a.setAttribute("target", "_blank");
      a.setAttribute("rel", "noopener noreferrer");
    });

  // UX nudge: flag likely scheme-less links in the source markdown
  const warningEl = document.getElementById("warning");
  if (warningEl) {
    const mdText = note.markdown || "";
    // very simple heuristic: "www." preceded by start/space/( and not already part of http(s)://
    const hasWwwNoScheme = /(^|[\s(])www\.[^\s)]+/i.test(mdText) && !/https?:\/\/\s*www\./i.test(mdText);

    warningEl.textContent = hasWwwNoScheme
      ? 'Some links look like "www.…" without https:// — please add the scheme.'
      : "";
  }

  setActive(note.slug);
}
```

If you want the warning to be “per-link” (e.g., list the exact `www.…` strings found), say so and I’ll tweak the regex to collect matches and display them nicely.

