If you want the “show the actual `www.…` strings” version, tweak it slightly so it’s accurate and doesn’t false-positive on things like `https://www.example.com` or `http://www.example.com`.

Key idea: **collect matches**, then **filter out ones that are immediately preceded by `http://` or `https://`** in the original markdown.

Drop-in snippet for the warning part (inside `if (warningEl) { ... }`), replacing your current boolean logic:

```js
const mdText = note.markdown || "";

// Find candidate "www." tokens (simple heuristic)
const re = /(^|[\s(])www\.[^\s)<>\]]+/gi;

const matches = [];
let m;

while ((m = re.exec(mdText)) !== null) {
  // m[0] includes the leading whitespace/paren if present
  const raw = m[0].trim().replace(/^[()]+/, "");

  // Look at the 12 chars immediately before the match start to see if it's "http://"/"https://"
  const start = m.index;
  const prefix = mdText.slice(Math.max(0, start - 12), start).toLowerCase();

  const hasSchemeJustBefore =
    prefix.endsWith("http://") || prefix.endsWith("https://");

  if (!hasSchemeJustBefore) matches.push(raw);
}

if (matches.length) {
  const unique = [...new Set(matches)];
  warningEl.textContent =
    unique.length === 1
      ? `Link appears to be missing https://: ${unique[0]}`
      : `Some links appear to be missing https://: ${unique.join(", ")}`;
} else {
  warningEl.textContent = "";
}
```

